[{:db/id 17592186203912 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-af0eb712-6f36-542d-b37c-e07c5fedf4a8" "question-6d34f2bd-1406-50c4-9338-867f1ecca34e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203702 :question/problem "Which of the following integers has a multiplicative inverse modulo $25?$\n\n- $7$\n\n- $10$\n\n- $12$" :question/worked-solution "An integer $a$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $n$ if and only if $a$ and $n$ are coprime, i.e., $\\textrm{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- $7$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $25$ since $\\textrm{gcd}(7,25)=1. \\:\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\n- $10$ does *not* have a multiplicative inverse modulo $25$ since $\\textrm{gcd}(10,25)=5\\neq1. \\:\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n- $12$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $25$ since $\\textrm{gcd}(12,25)=1. \\:\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203913 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4915f9fb-f03b-5b7e-8a32-e3e0683d3cad" "question-675f6fdb-8d6a-5e1a-8484-30f4c73cb80a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203703 :question/problem "Use trial and error to compute the multiplicative inverse of $8$ modulo $15.$" :question/worked-solution "The multiplicative inverse of $8$ modulo $15,$ denoted $8^{-1},$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n8 \\cdot x \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:15).\n$$\n\nNote that since $\\text{gcd}(8,15) = 1,$ an inverse element exists and is unique up to congruence modulo $15.$\n\nSince the modulus $n=15$ is small, we can find the inverse $x$ by computing exponents of $8$ (modulo $15$) until we get a result of $1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{2} & ≡8\\cdot 8≡64≡4\\cdot 15+4≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ {8}^{3} & ≡8\\cdot 4≡32≡2\\cdot 15+2≡2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ {8}^{4} & ≡8\\cdot 2≡16≡15+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $8 \\cdot 2 \\equiv 1 \\, (\\textrm{mod} \\:15).$ Therefore, the multiplicative inverse of $8$ modulo $15$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203914 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3e2bafbf-e373-5f9a-9cd6-8fbc664e6471" "question-18a6f0f2-d5f2-58c9-b7c0-478b95c5376b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203704 :question/problem "Use the extended Euclidean algorithm to compute the multiplicative inverse of $15$ modulo $86.$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, notice that $\\textrm{gcd}(15,86)=1.$ So, the multiplicative inverse of $15$ modulo $86$ exists.\n\nThe multiplicative inverse of $15$ modulo $86$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n15\\cdot x \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:86).\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $x,$ we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}86 & = & 15\\cdot 5 & + & 11 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 15 & = & 11\\cdot 1 & + & 4 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 11 & = & 4\\cdot 2 & + & 3 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 4 & = & 3\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11 & =86-15\\cdot 5 \\\\ 4 & =15-11 \\\\ 3 & =11-4\\cdot 2 \\\\ 1 & =4-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =4-3 \\\\  & =4-(11-4\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3-11 \\\\  & =(15-11)\\cdot 3-11 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 3-11\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 3-(86-15\\cdot 5)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 23-86\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 23+86\\cdot (-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result modulo $86,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15\\cdot 23+86\\cdot (-4) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,86) \\\\ 15\\cdot 23 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,86)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $15\\cdot 23\\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:86),$ which means that $15^{-1} \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: 86=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}23}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-af0eb712-6f36-542d-b37c-e07c5fedf4a8" :question/id #uuid "af0eb712-6f36-542d-b37c-e07c5fedf4a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-213407" :question/problem "Which of the following integers has a multiplicative inverse modulo $27?$\n\n- $11$\n- $16$\n- $23$" :question/worked-solution "An integer $a$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $n$ if and only if $a$ and $n$ are coprime, i.e., $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- $11$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $27$ since $\\text{gcd}(11,27)=1.\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $16$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $27$ since $\\text{gcd}(16,27)=1.\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $23$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $27$ since $\\text{gcd}(23,27)=1.\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a9802078-fa7f-52a5-a1e5-14125231ee58"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a9802078-fa7f-52a5-a1e5-14125231ee58" :answer-field/id #uuid "a9802078-fa7f-52a5-a1e5-14125231ee58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7aad8420-3c48-5474-98cb-d9e00f367a26" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b51e64bf-376c-52bb-ac74-2778610f3e9f" "answer-161735ec-56d4-5072-8a75-665310f43f19" "answer-f3f743a1-b3bd-5c44-85bc-086b14acdf8d" "answer-7aad8420-3c48-5474-98cb-d9e00f367a26" "answer-4c5e7ff7-af02-5c83-9cc7-4a20cdd2b462"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b51e64bf-376c-52bb-ac74-2778610f3e9f" :answer/id #uuid "b51e64bf-376c-52bb-ac74-2778610f3e9f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-161735ec-56d4-5072-8a75-665310f43f19" :answer/id #uuid "161735ec-56d4-5072-8a75-665310f43f19" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3f743a1-b3bd-5c44-85bc-086b14acdf8d" :answer/id #uuid "f3f743a1-b3bd-5c44-85bc-086b14acdf8d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7aad8420-3c48-5474-98cb-d9e00f367a26" :answer/id #uuid "7aad8420-3c48-5474-98cb-d9e00f367a26" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c5e7ff7-af02-5c83-9cc7-4a20cdd2b462" :answer/id #uuid "4c5e7ff7-af02-5c83-9cc7-4a20cdd2b462" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6d34f2bd-1406-50c4-9338-867f1ecca34e" :question/id #uuid "6d34f2bd-1406-50c4-9338-867f1ecca34e" :question/math-academy-id "q-213405" :question/problem "Which of the following integers has a multiplicative inverse modulo $36?$\n\n- $5$\n- $13$\n- $24$" :question/worked-solution "An integer $a$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $n$ if and only if $a$ and $n$ are coprime, i.e., $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- $5$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $36$ since $\\text{gcd}(5,36)=1.\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $13$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $36$ since $\\text{gcd}(13,36)=1.\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $24$ does *not* have a multiplicative inverse modulo $36$ since $\\text{gcd}(24,36)=12\\ne 1.\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d0aef841-96f7-59bb-8f67-62f78c7ca5b1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d0aef841-96f7-59bb-8f67-62f78c7ca5b1" :answer-field/id #uuid "d0aef841-96f7-59bb-8f67-62f78c7ca5b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d07425cc-ed1f-5b0a-8751-4e3973904462" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d07425cc-ed1f-5b0a-8751-4e3973904462" "answer-d66363e6-df16-5391-b2d0-6a090267e3a9" "answer-a41fc355-034f-5ded-bdfb-ebfb582a1bda" "answer-b03c9ec0-7f75-5d97-abbd-94b2869f47ad" "answer-b0c4fbb0-762c-5b67-afd1-1fb5e5110f8b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d07425cc-ed1f-5b0a-8751-4e3973904462" :answer/id #uuid "d07425cc-ed1f-5b0a-8751-4e3973904462" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d66363e6-df16-5391-b2d0-6a090267e3a9" :answer/id #uuid "d66363e6-df16-5391-b2d0-6a090267e3a9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a41fc355-034f-5ded-bdfb-ebfb582a1bda" :answer/id #uuid "a41fc355-034f-5ded-bdfb-ebfb582a1bda" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b03c9ec0-7f75-5d97-abbd-94b2869f47ad" :answer/id #uuid "b03c9ec0-7f75-5d97-abbd-94b2869f47ad" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0c4fbb0-762c-5b67-afd1-1fb5e5110f8b" :answer/id #uuid "b0c4fbb0-762c-5b67-afd1-1fb5e5110f8b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4915f9fb-f03b-5b7e-8a32-e3e0683d3cad" :question/id #uuid "4915f9fb-f03b-5b7e-8a32-e3e0683d3cad" :question/math-academy-id "q-213441" :question/problem "Use trial and error to compute the following residue.\n\n${3}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,11=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The multiplicative inverse of $3$ modulo $11,$ denoted ${3}^{-1},$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n3\\cdot x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11).\n$$\n\nNote that since $\\text{gcd}(3,11)=1,$ an inverse element exists and is unique up to congruence modulo $11.$\n\nSince the modulus $n=11$ is small, we can find the inverse $x$ by computing exponents of $3$ (modulo $11$) until we get a result of $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2} & ≡3\\cdot 3≡9 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {3}^{3} & ≡3\\cdot 9≡27≡2\\cdot 11+5≡5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {3}^{4} & ≡3\\cdot 5≡15≡11+4≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {3}^{5} & ≡3\\cdot 4≡12≡11+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $3\\cdot 4≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11).$ Therefore, the multiplicative inverse of $3$ modulo $11$ is $4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-25bf507a-8609-584a-8895-2544c9926a42"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-25bf507a-8609-584a-8895-2544c9926a42" :answer-field/id #uuid "25bf507a-8609-584a-8895-2544c9926a42" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-92df733c-9e8a-5b23-a107-168e6eed32b0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-92df733c-9e8a-5b23-a107-168e6eed32b0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-92df733c-9e8a-5b23-a107-168e6eed32b0" :answer/id #uuid "92df733c-9e8a-5b23-a107-168e6eed32b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-675f6fdb-8d6a-5e1a-8484-30f4c73cb80a" :question/id #uuid "675f6fdb-8d6a-5e1a-8484-30f4c73cb80a" :question/math-academy-id "q-213414" :question/problem "Use trial and error to compute the following residue.\n\n${3}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,5=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The multiplicative inverse of $3$ modulo $5,$ denoted ${3}^{-1},$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n3\\cdot x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5).\n$$\n\nNote that since $\\text{gcd}(3,5)=1,$ an inverse element exists and is unique up to congruence modulo $5.$\n\nSince the modulus $n=5$ is small, we can find the inverse $x$ by computing exponents of $3$ (modulo $5$) until we get a result of $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2} & ≡3\\cdot 3≡9≡5+4≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ {3}^{3} & ≡3\\cdot 4≡12≡2\\cdot 5+2≡2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ {3}^{4} & ≡3\\cdot 2≡6≡5+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $3\\cdot 2≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5).$ Therefore, the multiplicative inverse of $3$ modulo $5$ is $2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-df897945-d81d-5243-b778-8cc8364680a5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-df897945-d81d-5243-b778-8cc8364680a5" :answer-field/id #uuid "df897945-d81d-5243-b778-8cc8364680a5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-c5b0044e-5976-528d-9010-0418652be505" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c5b0044e-5976-528d-9010-0418652be505"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5b0044e-5976-528d-9010-0418652be505" :answer/id #uuid "c5b0044e-5976-528d-9010-0418652be505" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3e2bafbf-e373-5f9a-9cd6-8fbc664e6471" :question/id #uuid "3e2bafbf-e373-5f9a-9cd6-8fbc664e6471" :question/math-academy-id "q-213532" :question/problem "Fill in the blanks to make the following reasoning true.\n\nSince $\\text{gcd}(50,97)=$\n{{field-1}}, \nby the extended Euclidean algorithm, we have \n\n| $1\\,=\\,50\\,\\cdot ($\n{{field-2}}\n$)+\\,97\\,\\cdot ($\n{{field-3}}\n$).$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, ${50}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,97=$\n{{field-4}} (expressed as the smallest nonnegative residue)." :question/worked-solution "First of all, notice that $\\text{gcd}(50,97)=1.$ So, the multiplicative inverse of $50$ modulo $97$ exists.\n\nThe multiplicative inverse of $50$ modulo $97$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n50\\cdot x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,97).\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $x,$ we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}97 & = & 50\\cdot 1 & + & 47 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 50 & = & 47\\cdot 1 & + & 3 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 47 & = & 3\\cdot 15 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 3 & = & 2\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}47 & =97-50 \\\\ 3 & =50-47 \\\\ 2 & =47-3\\cdot 15 \\\\ 1 & =3-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =3-2 \\\\  & =3-(47-3\\cdot 15) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 16-47 \\\\  & =(50-47)\\cdot 16-47 \\\\  & =50\\cdot 16-47\\cdot 17 \\\\  & =50\\cdot 16-(97-50)\\cdot 17 \\\\  & =50\\cdot 33-97\\cdot 17 \\\\  & =50\\cdot 33+97\\cdot (-17)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result modulo $97,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50\\cdot 33+97\\cdot (-17) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,97) \\\\ 50\\cdot 33 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,97)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $50\\cdot 33≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,97),$ which means that ${50}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,97=33.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-89c3c4d5-f51f-5d05-ab6e-1c068b6cd4f9" "answer-field-2a0ab631-1b27-5aa9-aef1-a92dc676b3f2" "answer-field-e85832e1-b55f-56ed-bf6f-47527d9704c8" "answer-field-af0eb2c2-9bb3-53f5-8685-d1b8e9d768b7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-89c3c4d5-f51f-5d05-ab6e-1c068b6cd4f9" :answer-field/id #uuid "89c3c4d5-f51f-5d05-ab6e-1c068b6cd4f9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-160d5f45-9189-5260-9c2f-7a57f82aad3f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-160d5f45-9189-5260-9c2f-7a57f82aad3f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-160d5f45-9189-5260-9c2f-7a57f82aad3f" :answer/id #uuid "160d5f45-9189-5260-9c2f-7a57f82aad3f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2a0ab631-1b27-5aa9-aef1-a92dc676b3f2" :answer-field/id #uuid "2a0ab631-1b27-5aa9-aef1-a92dc676b3f2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-60a83021-eb0b-5bd6-adbb-9205cb87d642" :answer-field/choices ["answer-60a83021-eb0b-5bd6-adbb-9205cb87d642"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60a83021-eb0b-5bd6-adbb-9205cb87d642" :answer/id #uuid "60a83021-eb0b-5bd6-adbb-9205cb87d642" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "33" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e85832e1-b55f-56ed-bf6f-47527d9704c8" :answer-field/id #uuid "e85832e1-b55f-56ed-bf6f-47527d9704c8" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-5539c33b-1dce-5393-93a1-4064a290b70d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5539c33b-1dce-5393-93a1-4064a290b70d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5539c33b-1dce-5393-93a1-4064a290b70d" :answer/id #uuid "5539c33b-1dce-5393-93a1-4064a290b70d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-17" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-af0eb2c2-9bb3-53f5-8685-d1b8e9d768b7" :answer-field/id #uuid "af0eb2c2-9bb3-53f5-8685-d1b8e9d768b7" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-5b74eb17-4d64-5719-a0ec-bfc1de3209b3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5b74eb17-4d64-5719-a0ec-bfc1de3209b3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b74eb17-4d64-5719-a0ec-bfc1de3209b3" :answer/id #uuid "5b74eb17-4d64-5719-a0ec-bfc1de3209b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "33" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-18a6f0f2-d5f2-58c9-b7c0-478b95c5376b" :question/id #uuid "18a6f0f2-d5f2-58c9-b7c0-478b95c5376b" :question/math-academy-id "q-213530" :question/problem "Fill in the blanks to make the following reasoning true.\n\nSince $\\text{gcd}(55,258)=$\n{{field-1}}, \nby the extended Euclidean algorithm, we have \n\n| $1\\,=\\,55\\,\\cdot ($\n{{field-2}}\n$)+\\,258\\,\\cdot ($\n{{field-3}}\n$).$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, ${55}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,258=$\n{{field-4}} (expressed as the smallest nonnegative residue)." :question/worked-solution "First of all, notice that $\\text{gcd}(55,258)=1.$ So, the multiplicative inverse of $55$ modulo $258$ exists.\n\nThe multiplicative inverse of $55$ modulo $258$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n55\\cdot x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,258).\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $x,$ we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}258 & = & 55\\cdot 4 & + & 38 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 55 & = & 38\\cdot 1 & + & 17 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 38 & = & 17\\cdot 2 & + & 4 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 17 & = & 4\\cdot 4 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}38 & =258-55\\cdot 4 \\\\ 17 & =55-38 \\\\ 4 & =38-17\\cdot 2 \\\\ 1 & =17-4\\cdot 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =17-4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =17-(38-17\\cdot 2)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =17\\cdot 9-38\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =(55-38)\\cdot 9-38\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =55\\cdot 9-38\\cdot 13 \\\\  & =55\\cdot 9-(258-55\\cdot 4)\\cdot 13 \\\\  & =55\\cdot 61+258\\cdot (-13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result modulo $258,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}55\\cdot 61+258\\cdot (-13) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,258) \\\\ 55\\cdot 61 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,258)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $55\\cdot 61≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,258),$ which means that ${55}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,258=61.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dd8ed543-c520-541d-aaca-a83f9d308e3e" "answer-field-93f5dfb3-b0bd-54d4-9f0c-3a23cd8eb0e9" "answer-field-1a797595-f7b3-5fbe-8e81-bec203cecb7b" "answer-field-a57d0469-969b-5d66-a222-054cf955e5d0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dd8ed543-c520-541d-aaca-a83f9d308e3e" :answer-field/id #uuid "dd8ed543-c520-541d-aaca-a83f9d308e3e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-104e7c32-f9c9-51bc-ae0f-4c410215746f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-104e7c32-f9c9-51bc-ae0f-4c410215746f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-104e7c32-f9c9-51bc-ae0f-4c410215746f" :answer/id #uuid "104e7c32-f9c9-51bc-ae0f-4c410215746f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-93f5dfb3-b0bd-54d4-9f0c-3a23cd8eb0e9" :answer-field/id #uuid "93f5dfb3-b0bd-54d4-9f0c-3a23cd8eb0e9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f2f7e142-5fde-519c-99ea-9b33ca10f2d7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f2f7e142-5fde-519c-99ea-9b33ca10f2d7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2f7e142-5fde-519c-99ea-9b33ca10f2d7" :answer/id #uuid "f2f7e142-5fde-519c-99ea-9b33ca10f2d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "61" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1a797595-f7b3-5fbe-8e81-bec203cecb7b" :answer-field/id #uuid "1a797595-f7b3-5fbe-8e81-bec203cecb7b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-67ddaadf-b1b7-5b7d-8385-f1f708518b6d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-67ddaadf-b1b7-5b7d-8385-f1f708518b6d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67ddaadf-b1b7-5b7d-8385-f1f708518b6d" :answer/id #uuid "67ddaadf-b1b7-5b7d-8385-f1f708518b6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a57d0469-969b-5d66-a222-054cf955e5d0" :answer-field/id #uuid "a57d0469-969b-5d66-a222-054cf955e5d0" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7dd57479-5967-51e1-bdfa-54df7817cc48" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7dd57479-5967-51e1-bdfa-54df7817cc48"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7dd57479-5967-51e1-bdfa-54df7817cc48" :answer/id #uuid "7dd57479-5967-51e1-bdfa-54df7817cc48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "61" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186205241 :tutorial/content "The multiplicative inverse of an integer $a$ modulo $n$ is the unique number $x$ such that\n\n$$\na \\cdot x \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:n).\n$$\n\nThe word \"unique\" in the statement above means with respect to the inequality $0 \\leq x < n.$ \n\nFor example, $x={\\color{red}{4}}$ is the multiplicative inverse of $a={\\color{blue}{6}}$ modulo $23$ since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot 4 & ≡24 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,23) \\\\  & ≡23+1 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,23) \\\\  & ≡0+1 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,23) \\\\  & ≡1 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,23) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following: \n\n- We sometimes denote the multiplicative inverse of $a$ as $a^{-1}.$ So, ${\\color{blue}{6}}^{-1} = {\\color{red}{4}}$ in this example.\n\n- Be careful not confuse the notation $a^{-1}$ with the reciprocal of $a.$ In this context, both $a$ and $a^{-1}$ are *integers.*\n\n- The uniqueness of $a^{-1}$ is up to congruence modulo $n.$ For example, since ${\\color{red}{27}}\\equiv {\\color{red}{4}}\\:(\\textrm{mod }23),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27\\cdot 6 & ≡162 & (\\text{mod}\\,23) \\\\  & ≡7\\cdot 23+1 & (\\text{mod}\\,23) \\\\  & ≡7\\cdot 0+1 & (\\text{mod}\\,23) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,23) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are circumstances when a multiplicative inverse does not exist. To this end, we have the following theorem:\n\n*An integer $a$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $n$ if and only if $a$ and $n$ are coprime, i.e., $\\textrm{gcd}(a,n)=1.$*\n\nFor example:\n\n- We saw earlier that $6^{-1} = 4$ modulo $23.$ Indeed, $\\textrm{gcd}(6,23) = 1.$ Moreover, since $n=23$ is prime, *every* integer $1 \\leq x \\leq 22$ modulo $23$ has a multiplicative inverse. More on that in future lessons.\n\n- In contrast, the integer $a=2$ has no multiplicative inverse modulo $14$ since $\\textrm{gcd}(2,14) = 2\\neq 1.$ Intuitively, multiplying $a = 2$ by any integer $x$ modulo $14$ gives an even number, and taking the result modulo $14$ (also even) will never give us $1$ (an odd integer)." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186205255 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to calculate the multiplicative inverse of $3$ modulo $14.$ In other words, we want to find a number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n3 \\cdot x \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:14).\n$$\n\nFirstly, we note that since $\\textrm{gcd}(3,14) = 1,$ a unique multiplicative inverse exists. \n\nSince the modulus $n=14$ is fairly small, one way to efficiently compute the inverse is to use trial and error. To do this, we compute powers of $3$ and take the residue modulo $14$ until we get a result of $1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2} & ≡3\\cdot 3≡9 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\ {3}^{3} & ≡3\\cdot {3}^{2}≡3\\cdot 9≡27≡1\\cdot 14+13≡13 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\ {3}^{4} & ≡3\\cdot {3}^{3}≡3\\cdot 13≡39≡2\\cdot 14+11≡11 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\ {3}^{5} & ≡3\\cdot {3}^{4}≡3\\cdot 11≡33≡2\\cdot 14+5≡5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\ {3}^{6} & ≡3\\cdot {3}^{5}≡3\\cdot 5≡15≡1\\cdot 14+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,14)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the last line, we have\n\n$$\n3 \\cdot {\\color{magenta}{5}}   \\equiv 1\\qquad  (\\textrm{mod}\\:14).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the multiplicative inverse of $3$ modulo $14$ is ${\\color{magenta}{5}},$ and we can write \n\n$$\n3^{-1} = {\\color{magenta}{5}}\\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\:14).\n$$\n\n**Note:** We could get the same result by substituting $x=1,2,\\ldots, 13$ into \n\n$$\n3 \\cdot x \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:14)\n$$\n\nuntil we get the correct congruence relation. However, the exponentiation method described above typically allows us to find the solution with fewer trials since the multiplicative inverse of $a$ (if exists) must appear among the powers of $a$ modulo $n.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186205249 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose we want to compute the multiplicative inverse of $23$ modulo $79.$ \n\nSince $\\textrm{gcd}(23,79)=1,$ the inverse exists. However, the modulus $n=79$ is quite big, so finding the inverse by trial and error is inefficient. So, what do we do?\n\nTo answer this, recall that Bézout's identity states that there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that\n\n$$\n23u + 79v = \\textrm{gcd}(23,79).\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\textrm{gcd}(23,79)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n23u + 79v = 1.\n$$\n\nWe can find the integers $u$ and $v$ using the extended Euclidian algorithm.  \n\nApplying the Euclidian algorithm (we'll skip over the details for now), we find that $u=-24, v = 7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n23 \\cdot (-24) + 7 \\cdot 79 = 1.\n$$\n\nTo find the multiplicative inverse of $23,$ the key is to write this result modulo $79,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}23\\cdot (-24)+7\\cdot 79 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,79) \\\\ 23\\cdot (-24)+7\\cdot 0 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,79) \\\\ 23\\cdot (-24) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,79) \\\\ 23\\cdot (0-24) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,79) \\\\ 23\\cdot (79-24) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,79) \\\\ 23\\cdot 55 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,79)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $23 \\cdot 55 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:79).$ Therefore, $55$ is the multiplicative inverse of $23$ (modulo $79$).\n\nFinally, we can express this result using the residue function as follows:\n\n$$\n23^{-1} \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: 79 = 55\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204110 :activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
